      "abstract": "\n This essay approaches Russian Formalist theory from the perspective of Digital Humanities and corpus-based computational study of literature. In 2011, Franco Moretti and his coauthors from the Stanford Literary Lab labeled their research method quantitative formalism, positioning their methodology in deliberate opposition to Russian Formalism, which, in their accounting, was \u201cqualitative.\u201d In this essay, the author draws out a more extended genealogy of quantitative poetics by focusing on the methods developed by three members of the Moscow Linguistic Circle (a Moscow-based association of Russian Formalists)\u2014Boris Tomashevsky, Roman Jakobson, and Boris Yarkho. The essay goes on to offer a framework for understanding how the insights generated by Russian quantitative formalism might be understood and applied in the context of the present-day digital corpora.",
